      "writtenOn": "2024-12-24T02:40:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "But then I think you need a way to check whether the app has/does not have the permission at the start, otherwise the initial state will be incorrect.\n\nThere is a potential race though: if you first register the callback, and then\nuse synchronous methods to check the initial permission state, is there a guarantee that you will get all callbacks \"after\" the call to the getter (e.g. is the callback registration synchronous ?).\n\nIt might be worth checking with PermissionManager owners on the best way to do this.",
      "parentUuid": "342c43c7_87b091be",
      "revId": "91e10242fc4e12da4127850cfe99f180e9977ca0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4df3e3f0_298f141c",
        "filename": "service/src/com/android/server/connectivity/PermissionMonitor.java",
        "patchSetId": 27
      },
      "lineNbr": 289,
      "author": {
        "id": 1351609
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-27T17:59:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The code will get callbacks at least for changes that happen after the call to register the callback returns. But surely the code will be racy between the binder call to register the callback starts and the callback is actually received and registered on system server side. So if you want to build a reliable callback you should register callback first and fetch current data right after that.\n\nThat being said, this still have the raciness I mentioned earlier in emails, that apps that newly requested and got the permission granted may be able to attempt to use the granted permission before the permission change is delieved to your callback here.",
      "parentUuid": "74ada815_1c9e45bd",
      "revId": "91e10242fc4e12da4127850cfe99f180e9977ca0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "01771e25_349cb8bd",
        "filename": "service/src/com/android/server/connectivity/PermissionMonitor.java",
        "patchSetId": 27
      },
      "lineNbr": 289,
      "author": {
        "id": 3305446
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-30T15:11:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e you need a way to check whether the app has/does not have the permission at the start\n\nYes, we\u0027re planning to check all the packages and their permission state on initial boot to capture this initial state in the BPF program (some more details [here](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1NReL_Nppp9lGj0l-45pCPa5EbZk37JosU0YLf4vi5As/edit?tab\u003dt.0#bookmark\u003did.so3fbhv2b2ef)). FYI @sarup@google.com who has been working in the initial permission states, based on @zhanghai@google.com response above we should see how to properly synchronize these calls. Thanks for your input, Hai!\n\nIIUC, in most cases permission changes require user action such that by the time the user is able to change any permissions, the device has finished booting and the listener should be registered as part of intialization of the Connectivity Service. But will work with Sarup to come up with a more robust approach, as some cases (e.g. pregrant permissions) this is not guaranteed.",
      "parentUuid": "74ada815_1c9e45bd",
      "revId": "91e10242fc4e12da4127850cfe99f180e9977ca0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
